The '''1992–93 Division 1 season''' was the 55th since its establishment. [[Olympique de Marseille|Marseille]] finished in first with 53 points, but were stripped of their title due to a [[French football bribery scandal|bribery scandal]]. [[Paris Saint-Germain F.C.|Paris Saint-Germain]], who had finished second, refused to accept the title, resulting in no team being crowned champions for the season.

== Affaire VA-OM ==
{{main|French football bribery scandal}}
The European Cup was renamed the Champions League in [[1992–93 UEFA Champions League|1992–93]], and Marseille reached the final for the second time in three years. They defeated [[A.C. Milan|Milan]] to become the first French team to win a European Trophy.<ref>{{cite news|title=1992/93: French first for Marseille |url=https://www.uefa.com/competitions/UCL/history/Season=1992/intro.html |work=[[UEFA]] |date=26 May 1993 |access-date=7 December 2015 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070102183853/http://www.uefa.com/competitions/UCL/history/Season%3D1992/intro.html |archive-date=2 January 2007 }}</ref>

A [[French football bribery scandal|corruption scandal]] involving a league match against [[Valenciennes FC|Valenciennes]] emerged a few days before the Champions League final.<ref>{{cite news| title = Tapie Directly Implicated As Marseille Trial Opens| url = https://www.nytimes.com/1995/03/14/sports/14iht-tapie.html| work = [[International Herald Tribune]]| date = 14 March 1995| access-date = 1 February 2011}}</ref> It was alleged that club president [[Bernard Tapie]] had bribed Valenciennes to lose so that Olympique de Marseille would win the French League earlier, giving them more time to prepare for the Champions League final.<ref>{{cite news| title = Scandal leaves a stain on the white shirt of Marseille| url = https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-scandal-leaves-a-stain-on-the-white-shirt-of-marseille-allegations-of-matchfixing-of-francfilled-envelopes-buried-in-gardens-are-threatening-to-dethrone-the-kings-of-french-football-nick-bidwell-reports-1484695.html| work = [[The Independent]]| date = 13 July 1993| access-date = 28 October 2009}}</ref> Valenciennes players [[Christophe Robert]], [[Jorge Burruchaga]] and [[Jacques Glassmann]] claimed that Marseille midfielder [[Jean-Jacques Eydelie]] offered them [[French franc|₣]]250,000 to "take the foot off of the gas" in the May 20 match.<ref>{{cite news| title = From Glory to Disgrace: Soccer Saga Grips Marseille| url = https://www.nytimes.com/1993/07/12/news/12iht-soccer_7.html| work = [[The New York Times]]| date = 12 July 1993| access-date = 1 February 2011}}</ref> Marseille were later stripped of their league title and relegated to Division 2 by the [[French Football Federation]], while Tapie was forced to step down as the club's president. Marseille were not stripped of the Champions League, as the match in question was not in the competition, but were excluded from the [[1993–94 UEFA Champions League]],<ref>{{cite news
| title = Marseille: the French exception
| url = https://www.fifa.com/classicfootball/clubs/club=33192/index.html
| archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20090720115108/http://www.fifa.com/classicfootball/clubs/club=33192/index.html
| url-status = dead
| archive-date = July 20, 2009
| work = [[FIFA]]
| date = 13 August 2008
| access-date = 24 April 2010}}</ref> as well as regular reigning European champions match in [[1993 Intercontinental Cup]] and [[1993 European Super Cup]].

No winner was declared for the [[1992–93 Division 1|1992–1993]] season. The LFP allotted the title to Paris Saint-Germain but owners [[Canal+ (French TV channel)|Canal+]] refused it. The TV chain feared the reactions of their subscribers in [[Provence]] and threatened to withdraw from football completely if the title was awarded to PSG.{{fact|date=July 2024}} Ultimately the LFP decided that the 1993 title would remain unattributed. Canal+ refused to allow the club to participate in the following season's Champions League after Marseille's exclusion by [[UEFA]].<ref>Le PSG est vice-champion d'un championnat sans champion suite au déclassement de l'[[Olympique de Marseille]] (Affaire VA-OM). [[Canal+ (French TV channel)|Canal+]] a refusé le titre de champion pour le PSG car la chaîne cryptée ne voulait pas se fâcher avec ses abonnés de province. On lira sur cette affaire, Jean-François Pérès et Daniel Riolo, ''OM-PSG, PSG-OM. Les meilleurs ennemis, enquête sur une rivalité'', Paris, Mango Sport, 2003, p. 131–133: "Dans cette affaire, le PSG va plutôt choisir l'« intérêt supérieur de Canal+ » et s'obstiner dans son refus."</ref>

